{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNtWW58F5xWFz69hXWU9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I couldn't fix this from the code side. The cause is most likely in your `.env`, which I'm not allowed to read or edit.\n\n**What I found**\n- The startup check is `scripts/check-env.mjs`, which `npm run dev` and `npm run build` both run. It reads `.env` and exits if `GEMINI_API_KEY` is empty.\n- The old parser was fragile. It used `split('=')` with no trimming, so it could mishandle CRLF line endings, an `export ` prefix, quotes, or spaces. It also read `.env` relative to the current directory, so running it from another directory would miss the file.\n- I rewrote the script to use Node's built-in `util.parseEnv`. That handles all of those cases. It now finds `.env` relative to the repo root, and it accepts a `GEMINI_API_KEY` set in the shell environment.\n\n**Result**\n`npm run build` still reports the key as missing with the stricter parser. So the file is probably being read fine, but it has no usable value for that exact name. Your project rules and the policy gate say never to read or edit `.env`, so I didn't open it, and I can't tell you which of these it is. Check these yourself:\n- The line is exactly `GEMINI_API_KEY=<your key>`. The name must match exactly, including case and any typo or extra characters.\n- The value isn't empty. Copying `.env.example` over `.env` would leave it empty, because `.env.example` has `GEMINI_API_KEY=` with nothing after it.\n- The line isn't commented out with `#`.\n\nOnce the line is right, `npm run build` should print `built`.\n\nOne more problem: `src/lib/config.ts` reads `process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY`, but nothing in the repo loads `.env` into `process.env`. When the real app starts, run it with `node --env-file=.env ...` or use a framework that loads `.env` itself. Otherwise `geminiKey` will be `''` even when `.env` is correct."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":